5. Product lifecycle

The application remains a single, static, phone-first, provider-neutral, project-subject-neutral controller coordinating:
- the human operator;
- external production agents;
- independent reviewers;
- tool-capable environments;
- external systems;
- canonical project state;
- actual artifacts;
- deterministic gates;
- evidence;
- permanent defect and regression history.

The controlling lifecycle has two valid commit paths.

External semantic or production work:
human authority
→ raw intake and file custody
→ application reservation
→ exact prompt-file and handoff-package generation
→ external conversation or execution
→ authoritative response JSON file and returned-file selection
→ exact raw-byte staging
→ parse and validation
→ proposal
→ human acceptance only where the mutation contract requires human acceptance
→ canonical commit
→ deterministic derivation
→ stage gate
→ exact next action

Application-owned work:
validated application command
→ deterministic execution or derivation
→ atomic application-owned commit
→ stage gate
→ exact next action

Final delivery:
product eligibility
→ delivery-candidate freeze and inspection
→ exact artifact identity verification
→ evidence-chain closure
→ atomic terminal determination
→ authorized export or share action
→ delivery-attempt evidence at its actual epistemic basis

No application-owned calculation is converted into a human-approved semantic proposal merely to fit a single lifecycle pattern.

The complete workflow covers:
- initial human-authority intake;
- source inventory;
- source research;
- requirement compilation;
- requirement resolution;
- verification design;
- failure-test design;
- production-instruction creation;
- preflight;
- candidate freeze;
- ten independent executions;
- independent verification;
- cross-run comparison;
- root-cause analysis;
- regression creation;
- root-cause correction;
- repeated corrected iterations;
- convergence;
- unchanged confirmation;
- baseline freeze;
- finished-product generation;
- deterministic verification;
- meaning-based verification;
- adversarial verification;
- final-representation inspection;
- process/product reconciliation;
- release;
- byte-identity verification;
- evidence-chain proof;
- permanent failure preservation.

